Fan review complete and saved to `docs/content-analysis/perspective-reviews/Michael-Jordan/2026-08-14_020004/fan.md` (SHA verified against context.json and the supplied argument).

**Verdict:** revise — trust strained, value high, delight clear_hit. The piece passes the fan delight test ("I knew those moments, but I had never connected them that way"), but two canon errors need surgical fixes before a basketball-literate reader sees it.

**Blockers: 2**
- **FAN-B1:** The coin-pitching shrug is inverted — the documented Last Dance scene has security guard Wozniak beating Jordan and shrugging at *him* (packet-confirmed contradiction). The true version actually strengthens the thesis.
- **FAN-B2:** The grievance ledger places the Bryon Russell callback "at center circle in the 1996 Finals" — the Bulls played Seattle in 1996 (the draft's own Father's Day section says so) and Utah in 1997. The on-file HOF transcript shows the year-slip is Jordan's own misremembering ("we played Utah 96"), which the draft repeats as fact — fixable as a correction or, better, as the table's middle column catching its own narrator.

**Strongest hit:** the Bird paragraph — "Praise has no shelf in the archive. The machine files threats, and when inventory runs low, it prints more." A quote every fan knows, made newly load-bearing.

**Highest-priority concern:** the grievance ledger skips the most famous *real* grievance in the canon — the 1991 Pistons walk-off and its 2020 coda, where Jordan was handed Isiah Thomas's explanation on an iPad and rejected the correction on camera ([CBS Sports, 2020](https://www.cbssports.com/nba/news/the-last-dance-michael-jordan-calls-isiah-thomas-an-a-hole-for-walking-off-court-in-bulls-pistons-series)). One ~35-word row completes the table's own argument.
